          "parts": [
            {
              "text": "When acting as an executor in Queensland, obtaining legal representation helps ensure you comply with the *Succession Act 1981 (Qld)* and the *Uniform Civil Procedure Rules 1999 (QCPR)*, particularly regarding advertising requirements, the Supreme Court probate application, and protecting yourself against personal liability.\n\nSeveral reputable law firms based in or serving the **Ipswich** area specialise in Queensland probate and deceased estate administration:\n\n---\n\n### 1. Established Local Ipswich Firms\n\n*   **Fallu McMillan Lawyers** (Brisbane Street, Ipswich)  \n    A longstanding Ipswich firm operating for over 80 years. They have a dedicated wills and estates practice that routinely guides executors through Supreme Court Grants of Probate, Letters of Administration (if there is no valid will), estate accounting, and asset transmission.\n*   **McNamara Law** (Ipswich, with offices in Springfield and Brisbane)  \n    One of the largest legal practices in the region. McNamara Law has a substantial estate administration team that assists executors in navigating financial institutions, superannuation death benefits, property transfers via Titles Queensland, and creditor notifications.\n*   **Richard Zande & Associates** (Ipswich CBD)  \n    Practising in Ipswich for over six decades, this firm has deep roots in the local community and frequently assists executors with formal probate applications, estate asset collection, and beneficiary distributions.\n*   **Rose Jensen Kent Lawyers** (Ipswich)  \n    A long-established Ipswich firm with extensive experience in estate administration, probate processing, and handling complex or contested estates.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Wills & Estates Boutiques & Western Suburbs Practices\n\n*   **Jacaranda Law** (Ipswich)  \n    A boutique practice focusing solely on wills, elder law, probate, and estate administration. Because they specialise purely in succession law, they provide targeted assistance for executors needing fixed-scope help (e.g., just the probate grant) or full administration.\n*   **Keane Legal** (Ipswich & Greater Springfield)  \n    Focuses on succession law, including executor advice, probate and reseal applications, and estate distribution throughout the western corridor.\n*   **Stanford Legal** (Ipswich & Springfield)  \n    Provides deceased estate administration services specifically tailored for executors, assisting with Supreme Court filings, resolving estate debts, and final distributions.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Larger Regional / Statewide Practices with Local Ipswich Offices\n\n*   **Turner Freeman Lawyers** (Ipswich Office)  \n    A prominent Queensland firm with a local presence in Ipswich. Their succession law team is widely recognised (including by *Doyle’s Guide*) and handles both straightforward probate grants and high-value, contentious, or multi-jurisdictional estates.\n\n---\n\n### Key Questions to Ask When Selecting a Firm\n\nWhen you reach out to these firms as an executor, consider asking:\n\n1. **Fee Structure:** Do they charge a **fixed fee** for the Grant of Probate application, or do they bill on an hourly/scale-fee basis? *(Executor legal costs are generally payable directly from the estate's funds, not your own pocket.)*\n2. **Scope of Engagement:** Can they assist on a **limited retainer** (e.g., obtaining the Grant of Probate only while you handle asset collection), or do they manage full administration from start to finish?\n3. **Timeframes & Deadlines:** Can they advise you on Queensland’s statutory limitation windows—notably waiting **6 months** for family provision notices and **9 months** before distributing assets without personal risk under Queensland law?",
              "thoughtSignature": ""
            }
          ],
